**Convention 6.3 (inherited positive-definite branch and transition).** For real $C\succ0$, RG already supplies the same scalar relaxation. The unique positive generalized root $\lambda_0$ is the reciprocal of the unique positive eigenvalue of $C^{-1/2}BC^{-1/2}$. Its slack $C-\lambda_0B$ is positive of rank two. Normalize its real kernel $v_0$ with positive $D$ coordinate and put $b_0=v_0^TBv_0>0$. If $b_0>k$, the relaxation is uniquely minimized at $(k/b_0)v_0v_0^T$, value $k\lambda_0$. This strictly unsaturated case has no general maximal cap theorem here.

If $b_0=k$, put $v=v_0,\lambda=\lambda_0,\mu=0$. If $b_0<k$, the simple top branch for $\lambda>\lambda_0$ has the unique selector $b=k$, with $\mu>0$, exactly as in (PG.27). The strict derivative proof applies until $b=1$; a decoupled $D$ would instead have $b_0=1$ and is excluded from this case. These are inherited RG values and vectors, not new positive-definite spectral results. Formula (PG.30) proves unique $H=P$ also at the transition $\mu=0$: the rank-two slack forces $H=tP$, and positive $\lambda$ forces $tk=k$, hence $t=1$. The equality theorem below applies to all these saturated cases, including the direct singular case.

